  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Gelof and the bullpen held it together when early damage could have sunk them.",
    "what_this_means": "What matters here isn't that they won 7\u20136. It's that they won it with a starter who lasted 2.2 innings and couldn't find the strike zone. Kade Morris looked like he wouldn't survive the second inning. Five runs in 2.2 IP, an ERA that's rotted to 10.90. But Brady Basso came in and threw three scoreless innings, followed by two more relievers who kept things flat until Harris closed it out. That's not a bullpen saving the day. That's the bullpen defining the day. Gelof's two home runs and two RBI gave them the firepower to survive, and they converted two runners-in-scoring-position situations into runs when they needed to most. The margin was always thin. Never ahead by more than three, trailed early. But they didn't fold when the pitcher couldn't hold it. This is a team that can win ugly when the bullpen and a hitter show up at once.",
    "what_to_watch": "Seattle comes to town tonight with a .230 average, and Jeffrey Springs gets the call. That's the kind of matchup that rewards what the A's just proved. They can survive a starter's bad day if someone behind him is ready to answer.",
